## Code modularity — search before you add, extract before you duplicate

A short rule that applies to every code change you make, not just VCO ones.

**Before writing any new function, class, helper, or script, search for existing code that does the same thing.** Use `hybrid_search` first (conceptual queries hit the KG and project docs), then `search_code_graph` (by purpose), then `grep` for exact symbols. If you find one or more existing implementations of the same capability:

1. **Do not add a third implementation alongside the existing duplicates.** Each generation of duplication compounds — the next editor will face N+1 copies to keep in sync, and the bug-fix you make in your copy will silently drift from theirs.
2. **Extract all existing implementations into one shared module** (`vco_lib/<topic>.py` for Python helpers, `<crate>/src/services/<topic>.rs` for Rust, `scripts/lib/<topic>.sh` for shell). Pick the cleanest variant as the seed and reconcile differences against the call-sites.
3. **Migrate the existing call-sites** to import the shared helper.
4. **Then add your new call-site** that also calls the shared helper.

Steps 2–3 cost time you didn't budget for. They are still cheaper than letting the duplication grow another generation. Track J of this cycle exists specifically to clean up duplications that earlier cycles deferred; do not add to its queue.

**Same principle for file size**: if your change would add more than ~50 contiguous lines to a file already past ~5,000 lines (`install.py` is past 25,000 today), extract the new logic into a new module instead. Mega-scripts make every future audit and refactor more expensive.

When extraction is genuinely hard (the new logic depends on local mutable state in a long sequential flow), extract the pure portion to a helper module and leave a thin orchestration shim in the original file. Document the split in a comment so the next editor does not merge them back.

**A few more maintainability rules** (apply to every change):
- **One concern, one home.** If logic is needed in 2+ call-sites, write it as a single function/module called from each — never inline a second copy. The moment a second caller appears for inlined logic, refactor it into one function first.
- **Single responsibility.** Keep functions small and named for what they decide/do; push side-effecting I/O to the edges so the core stays unit-testable.
- **Conservative defaults on best-effort paths.** When an operation can't positively confirm its precondition (process identity, file freshness, remote state), do nothing rather than guess — soft-fail with a log line, never crash the caller.
- **Share, don't mirror, cross-language logic (A>B>C).** When the same rule must exist in more than one language, eliminate divergence at the SOURCE, in this priority: **(A) shared code** — one implementation (Python SSOT) called cross-language via a tiny CLI/subprocess (`python -m vco_lib.<module>`), the default wherever latency allows (user-action-triggered, ms-scale paths); **(B) shared config** — one committed rule table both sides PARSE, when a per-call subprocess is genuinely too costly (a hot loop / per-keystroke / per-env-key path) or no interpreter is available; **(C) mirror** — last resort only, for pure control-flow that resists A and B, with the data still extracted to config so only a thin wrapper differs, and a parity test + "must match <other file>" comment locking it. Never add a fresh mirror when A or B is reachable. **And loud-fail, never silent-fallback, on vco_lib imports:** vco_lib is part of every healthy VCO install (editable-installed into the resolved venv), so an `import vco_lib` that fails means a BROKEN install — surface it loudly (clear stderr + non-zero exit) so it reaches the user, never a quiet inline-copy degrade that masks the breakage.
- **Test the decision, not just the happy path.** Every new branch that gates a destructive action (stop/kill/delete/overwrite) gets a unit test for both the act and the leave-alone case.

**Hard rule**: never push a tagged release if there is ANY known unresolved issue at tag time. "Known" means anything that has been (a) reported by an audit/review, (b) flagged by CI on the current branch, (c) reproduced as a bug in this or a prior cycle and not yet closed, or (d) explicitly written down as a backlog/follow-up item targeting "next version."

**Triggers this rule (do NOT do these things)**:
- "We'll fix the smoke test in the next release." → **No.** Fix it in this release or explicitly delete the assertion.
- "Backlog for v0.2.X+1: …" → **No.** Either close in v0.2.X or document a deliberate scope decision the user has explicitly approved.
- "Push v0.2.X tag now while CI is still red on installer-smoke — it's not gating Release anyway." → **No.** Red gates block tagging, period. Even non-Release-gating workflows.
- "We can clean up the dead code/imports/TODO later." → **No.** Either delete it now or open a non-deferred follow-up that lands within the same tag's cycle.

**What "no deferred fixes" requires before every tag**:
1. `UPDATE_DEFERRED.md` is EMPTY (no rows queued for next release).
2. `gh run list --limit 30` on the release branch + main shows zero red conclusions on ANY workflow (Release, CI, installer-smoke, manifest-validate, hook-os-parity, codeql, etc.) — including informational ones.
3. No `TODO(next-release)` / `FIXME(v0.2.X+1)` / `XXX` annotations have been added since the last tag without being closed.
4. CHANGELOG.md `[Unreleased]` section is EMPTY (all entries moved into the tagged version block).
5. Every audit/review artifact under `.claude/context/reviews/` for the current cycle has been reconciled (no `IN-SCOPE` items still pending; all `OUT-OF-SCOPE` items have explicit user-approval rationale recorded inline).

**Why this rule exists**: v0.2.40 shipped 18 agent landings + R3-R5 follow-ups all flagged "ops follow-up needed" / "container-side TODO" / "dead code, cleanup next release". v0.2.41 was a CI-gate hotfix that ALSO shipped with installer-smoke still red + Weaviate-bootstrap still failing + 4 deferred items. Each chained bugfix release erodes the user-facing credibility of every prior tag. User correction 2026-05-31: "do not push a release if you are deferring fixing some issue with the release. Fix first then push."

**How to apply when scope feels too big**: ask the user "this would push v0.2.X out by N days — accept defer with explicit rationale OR cut scope from this release?" Then act on whichever they choose. Don't decide unilaterally to defer.

**Cross-reference**: see the `feedback_no_release_with_deferred_fixes.md` memory note in your own auto-memory directory (`~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/memory/`).

