## Context Management

**Two-Layer Memory**:
- `MEMORY.md` (`~/.claude/projects/.../memory/`) — Claude's self-written operational notes. Loaded into the system prompt every session by the Claude Code memory feature, which **hard-truncates to the first 200 lines / ~25 KB — silently dropping everything after** (a harness behaviour, not configurable from VCO). So keep it a concise one-line-per-entry index and put detail in topic files (`debugging.md`, etc.) — topic files are loaded on demand and are NOT capped. Stable patterns, recurring bug fixes, key paths, user preferences. NOT in git. Edit via `/memory` or ask Claude directly ("remember that...").
- `.claude/CONTEXT_STATE.md` — Active working memory (250–350 lines, max 500). Current task, recent progress, next steps, active blockers. Update during work, not just at end. Unlike MEMORY.md it is **never truncated**: the VCO `context-size-check.sh` hook emits only a soft *warning* (at 500 lines) prompting a trim — it never drops content.
- **Per-session state (concurrent chats)**: `.claude/CONTEXT_STATE.md` is the shared project rollup. When you run multiple long-lived chats against the same project at once (e.g. a main chat + an RL chat), each can keep its OWN `.claude/context/CONTEXT_STATE_<session_id>.md` so they don't clobber the shared rollup (last-writer-wins). The size-check / diff-inject / compact-reinject hooks pick it up automatically once it exists; single-session projects never create one and pay nothing.

| MEMORY.md | CONTEXT_STATE.md |
|---|---|
| "use pnpm not npm", solved recurring bugs, stable architecture facts, service URLs | Current sprint goal, recent progress ✅, next steps, open blockers |

**Other Context**:
- `.claude/context/archive/` — Completed tasks
- `.claude/context/plans/` — Active plans (referenced, not auto-loaded)

**Token Efficiency**:
- Parallel tool calls: Read/Grep multiple files in a single message.
- Read files directly for files <150 lines; use `offset`/`limit` for larger ones.
- Cache mentally for 20–30 minutes — don't re-read the same file repeatedly within a session.
- Limit shell output: `| head -30` or `2>&1 | tail -20`.
- Skip echoing after writes; trust the tool result.
- Spawn agents for multi-file ops (cap at 3 parallel).

**Large Documents (>20 pages)**: Skim ToC/structure first, then targeted section reads; note discoveries as you go.

